From: =?utf-8?b?TWljaGFsIEtvdXRuw70gPG1rb3V0bnlAc3VzZS5jb20+?=
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 15:27:29 +0200
Subject: af_unix: Peek the queue synchronized
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit
Patch-mainline: Never, SLES conservative fix
References: CVE-2026-64109 bsc#1273748

The skb_peek_tail() doesn't guarantee any stability of the returned
sk_buff *. The unix_stream_data_wait() needs to check possible change of
size of the peek'd SKB (as that denotes splicing happened there),
however, the tail->len is potentially unsafe when the returned SKB
happened to be dequeud and free'd.

Upstream commit be309f8eae8b ("af_unix: Fix UAF read of tail->len in
unix_stream_data_wait()") avoided this UaF by not checking tail->len at all
because splicing is implemented by adding new SKBs to the queue in the newer
kernels.

The conservative fix for this race leading to UaF is to take the
sk_receive_queue.lock which ensures that no one should manipulate with the
peek'd SKB and we can safely check its ->len.
The lock can be nested inside unix_state_lock() but there may be slight
performance impact for the MSG_PEEK users.

Signed-off-by: Michal Koutný <mkoutny@suse.com>
---
 net/unix/af_unix.c | 10 ++++++++--
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/net/unix/af_unix.c b/net/unix/af_unix.c
index 60784fecdd133..ec8bc2b39aa3e 100644
--- a/net/unix/af_unix.c
+++ b/net/unix/af_unix.c
@@ -2450,10 +2450,16 @@ static long unix_stream_data_wait(struct sock *sk, long timeo,
 	for (;;) {
 		prepare_to_wait(sk_sleep(sk), &wait, state);
 
+		spin_lock(&sk->sk_receive_queue.lock);
 		tail = skb_peek_tail(&sk->sk_receive_queue);
 		if (tail != last ||
-		    (tail && tail->len != last_len) ||
-		    sk->sk_err ||
+		    (tail && tail->len != last_len)) {
+			spin_unlock(&sk->sk_receive_queue.lock);
+			break;
+		}
+		spin_unlock(&sk->sk_receive_queue.lock);
+
+		if (sk->sk_err ||
 		    (sk->sk_shutdown & RCV_SHUTDOWN) ||
 		    signal_pending(current) ||
 		    !timeo)

